WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.436
A useful parcel photo shows the evidence someone else needs to check.

00:00:05.436 --> 00:00:14.713
Capture Loop turns a photograph into a concrete next step, using OpenCV five, AWS, and a tool-using agent.

00:00:16.378 --> 00:00:19.330
Start with this cropped shipping label.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:25.949
The agent calls the image tool, reads the missing-boundary result, and asks for a complete view.

00:00:25.949 --> 00:00:30.014
That request stays in the history for the next photograph.

00:00:31.778 --> 00:00:34.967
Here is a separate GLS parcel.

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:40.099
The cloud measurement finds all four corners and prepares a straightened view.

00:00:40.099 --> 00:00:43.243
The agent now opens field review.

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:46.849
The original stays beside its derived image.

00:00:48.618 --> 00:00:51.860
The reviewer supplies the visible value.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:58.583
In this scripted demonstration, I compare the handwritten date, enter it, and save the confirmation.

00:00:58.583 --> 00:01:04.689
The export carries the photo history, measurements, tool calls, and review together.

00:01:06.642 --> 00:01:12.028
The browser sends the photo to a private OpenCV Lambda through the station.

00:01:12.028 --> 00:01:18.213
The model receives measurements and the previous request, then executes the next action.

00:01:18.213 --> 00:01:23.068
Four selected parcel examples document the current domain checks.

00:01:25.026 --> 00:01:27.939
Open the live station on your phone.

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:33.702
Try the supplied samples, or one flat label against a contrasting surface.

00:01:33.702 --> 00:01:39.452
Tell us which capture instruction helped, and which detail still needed a clearer view.

